below.

This repository is published so that anyone can check how Ringer Vault
protects the data in it. It is source-available for review. It is not open
source.

1. What you may do, free of charge

   a. Read, download and keep copies of this repository.
   b. Build it and run its tests on your own computers and devices.
   c. Change it privately to investigate, test or demonstrate a security
      issue.
   d. Quote reasonable excerpts in security reviews, research, articles,
      talks and vulnerability reports, with a link back to this repository.

2. What you may not do without written permission

   a. Distribute this code, or a changed version of it, other than excerpts
      under 1(d).
   b. Use any part of it in another product or service, paid or free.
   c. Publish an app, extension or service built from it.
   d. Use the Ringer Vault name or logo for anything that is not Ringer
      Vault.

3. Reports and contributions

   Security reports are welcome at ringervault@gmail.com. If you send a
   change to the code, you allow the copyright holder to use it in Ringer
   Vault, without payment, worldwide and for all time.

4. Third-party code

   Third-party code in this repository keeps its own licence, which takes
   precedence for that code. This includes hash-wasm (MIT,
   extension/src/vendor/hash-wasm-LICENSE) and the Public Suffix List data
   (Mozilla Public License 2.0, noted in the files that hold it).

5. No warranty

   This repository is provided "as is", without warranty of any kind. The
   copyright holder is not liable for any claim, damage or other liability
   arising from it or from its use.

6. Ending

   If you break these terms, your permission under section 1 ends.
